1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,640
 Imagine I ask an AI this question.

2
00:00:02,640 --> 00:00:04,760
 What is the capital of France?

3
00:00:04,760 --> 00:00:07,600
 To you these are words, but to a computer,

4
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:09,520
 the words capital, France,

5
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:12,720
 and even the question itself cannot exist as language.

6
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:14,760
 Computers understand numbers,

7
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:16,960
 so somewhere inside an AI system,

8
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:19,400
 this sentence has to become mathematics.

9
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:21,560
 And one of the most important mathematical objects

10
00:00:21,560 --> 00:00:25,360
 making that possible is surprisingly simple, a vector.

11
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:27,440
 So what exactly is a vector?

12
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:29,720
 You may remember vectors from physics,

13
00:00:29,720 --> 00:00:31,960
 an arrow pointing in some direction.

14
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:34,440
 For example, this vector might move three units

15
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:36,800
 to the right and two units upward.

16
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:40,120
 We can write it as two numbers, three, two.

17
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:43,080
 The first number tells us how far to move horizontally.

18
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:45,720
 The second tells us how far to move vertically,

19
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:48,120
 but there is another way to think about a vector.

20
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:50,600
 A vector is simply a list of numbers,

21
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:54,840
 three, two, or perhaps three, two, five,

22
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:56,800
 or a thousand different numbers.

23
00:00:56,800 --> 00:00:59,520
 And this simple idea gives artificial intelligence

24
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:01,280
 something extremely powerful

25
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:03,640
 a way to turn meaning into geometry.

26
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:06,000
 Let's start with a tiny example.

27
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,920
 Suppose we have three words, cat, dog, and car.

28
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:11,880
 We want a computer to somehow understand

29
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:13,560
 that cat and dog are related,

30
00:01:13,560 --> 00:01:15,160
 while car is very different.

31
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:18,520
 Imagine giving each word two numerical properties.

32
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:21,160
 The first represents how much the object is related

33
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:22,320
 to animals.

34
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:25,640
 The second represents how much it is related to machines.

35
00:01:25,640 --> 00:01:29,120
 A cat might become 0.9, 0.1.

36
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:33,080
 A dog might become 0.85, 0.1.

37
00:01:33,080 --> 00:01:37,240
 And a car might become 0.05, 0.95.

38
00:01:37,240 --> 00:01:39,360
 Now place these vectors on a graph.

39
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:41,120
 Something interesting happens.

40
00:01:41,120 --> 00:01:43,200
 Cat and dog appear close together.

41
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:45,200
 Car appears somewhere else.

42
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:47,360
 We have taken something abstract meaning

43
00:01:47,360 --> 00:01:49,080
 and turned it into distance.

44
00:01:49,080 --> 00:01:51,400
 This is the basic intuition behind something called

45
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:52,360
 an embedding.

46
00:01:52,360 --> 00:01:55,200
 And embedding represents information as vectors.

47
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:58,280
 Real AI systems don't use just two dimensions.

48
00:01:58,280 --> 00:02:00,480
 They might use hundreds or thousands.

49
00:02:00,480 --> 00:02:03,360
 So instead of representing the word cat with two numbers,

50
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:08,640
 it might look something like 0.17, negative 0.42,

51
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:13,800
 0.81, 0.06, and hundreds of other values.

52
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:16,840
 Individually, these numbers usually don't have simple meanings

53
00:02:16,840 --> 00:02:18,640
 like animal or machine.

54
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:21,920
 But together, their position captures useful patterns.

55
00:02:21,920 --> 00:02:24,200
 And this is where vectors become fascinating.

56
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:27,120
 Suppose two vectors point in similar directions.

57
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:29,320
 Mathematically, we can measure their similarity

58
00:02:29,320 --> 00:02:31,480
 using something called the dot product.

59
00:02:31,480 --> 00:02:34,600
 Take two vectors, multiply their matching components,

60
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:36,160
 then add the results.

61
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:38,360
 If the vectors point in similar directions,

62
00:02:38,360 --> 00:02:40,320
 the dot product tends to be large.

63
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:42,320
 If they point in very different directions,

64
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:43,960
 it tends to be smaller.

65
00:02:43,960 --> 00:02:45,960
 AI systems can use ideas like this

66
00:02:45,960 --> 00:02:47,800
 to compare pieces of information.

67
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:50,440
 So when you type the cat chased the mouse,

68
00:02:50,440 --> 00:02:53,320
 the model doesn't see words floating in empty space.

69
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:55,640
 It works with numerical representations

70
00:02:55,640 --> 00:02:58,840
 that can be compared, transformed, and combined.

71
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:01,200
 But vectors aren't limited to words.

72
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:03,520
 And image can become vectors too.

73
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:07,440
 Imagine an image of a dog that first the images just pixels.

74
00:03:07,440 --> 00:03:11,120
 Every pixel contains numbers describing brightness and color.

75
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:13,120
 A neural network processes those numbers

76
00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:14,760
 through layer after layer.

77
00:03:14,760 --> 00:03:17,240
 Early layers might respond to simple features,

78
00:03:17,240 --> 00:03:19,440
 edges, lines, corners.

79
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:22,120
 Later layers can represent more complex patterns.

80
00:03:22,120 --> 00:03:25,880
 Eyes, ears, fur, faces, objects.

81
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:29,680
 Eventually, the image becomes an internal vector representation.

82
00:03:29,680 --> 00:03:32,120
 So an image of a dog and the word dog

83
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:34,400
 can both be represented mathematically.

84
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:36,520
 This is what makes systems that connect text

85
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:38,000
 and images possible.

86
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,240
 Vectors give different kinds of information,

87
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:42,160
 a common mathematical language.

88
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:44,680
 And there's another reason AI loves vectors.

89
00:03:44,680 --> 00:03:46,160
 Computers are incredibly good

90
00:03:46,160 --> 00:03:49,000
 at performing operations on large arrays of numbers.

91
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:50,800
 Suppose we have one vector.

92
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:53,120
 We can multiply it by another mathematical object

93
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:54,400
 called a matrix.

94
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:57,920
 A matrix can rotate a vector, stretch it, compress it,

95
00:03:57,920 --> 00:04:00,960
 or transform it into a completely different space.

96
00:04:00,960 --> 00:04:04,040
 Inside a neural network, this happens constantly.

97
00:04:04,040 --> 00:04:06,680
 Vector goes in, matrix transformation.

98
00:04:06,680 --> 00:04:08,280
 New vector comes out.

99
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:11,440
 Then another transformation, and another, and another.

100
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:14,480
 What we call a neural network is at a mathematical level,

101
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:18,120
 largely a huge sequence of transformations applied to vectors.

102
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:20,960
 Now let's return to language. Imagine the sentence.

103
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:23,880
 The animal didn't cross the street because it was tired.

104
00:04:23,880 --> 00:04:25,680
 What does it refer to?

105
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:26,960
 Probably the animal.

106
00:04:26,960 --> 00:04:29,000
 But how could a machine determine that?

107
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:31,960
 Modern language models use a mechanism called attention.

108
00:04:31,960 --> 00:04:34,120
 And vectors are at the center of it.

109
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:36,440
 Each word is converted into vectors.

110
00:04:36,440 --> 00:04:39,200
 Then the model creates different versions of those vectors,

111
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:41,880
 usually called queries, keys, and values.

112
00:04:41,880 --> 00:04:44,000
 The model compares them mathematically.

113
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,640
 If two pieces of information are strongly related,

114
00:04:46,640 --> 00:04:48,960
 their vectors produce a stronger connection.

115
00:04:48,960 --> 00:04:51,640
 So the representation for it can pay more attention

116
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:53,480
 to animal than to street.

117
00:04:53,480 --> 00:04:56,960
 This process happens again and again across many layers.

118
00:04:56,960 --> 00:04:59,400
 Millions or billions of vector operations

119
00:04:59,400 --> 00:05:02,400
 eventually produce the model's understanding of your sentence,

120
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:04,640
 even the output works with vectors.

121
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:07,480
 Suppose the model is trying to predict the next word.

122
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:08,800
 The sky is.

123
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:11,600
 Inside the model is a vector representing everything

124
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:13,320
 it has understood so far.

125
00:05:13,320 --> 00:05:17,080
 That vector is transformed into scores for possible next words.

126
00:05:17,080 --> 00:05:19,000
 Blue might receive a high score.

127
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:20,680
 Clear might receive another.

128
00:05:20,680 --> 00:05:22,920
 Falling might receive a much lower one.

129
00:05:22,920 --> 00:05:25,120
 Those scores become probabilities.

130
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:26,680
 One word is selected.

131
00:05:26,680 --> 00:05:28,400
 Then the process repeats.

132
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:31,760
 So when an AI generates a paragraph behind every word

133
00:05:31,760 --> 00:05:34,200
 are enormous numbers of vector calculations.

134
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:37,320
 But perhaps the most surprising thing about vectors is this.

135
00:05:37,320 --> 00:05:39,200
 They can capture relationships.

136
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:43,080
 In a good vector space, similar ideas often appear near each other.

137
00:05:43,080 --> 00:05:44,880
 Paris may be close to France.

138
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:47,160
 Tokyo may relate similarly to Japan.

139
00:05:47,160 --> 00:05:49,880
 Words involving emotions may form patterns.

140
00:05:49,880 --> 00:05:52,120
 Programming concepts may form others.

141
00:05:52,120 --> 00:05:53,800
 The geometry isn't perfect.

142
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:56,000
 And it isn't the same as human understanding.

143
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:57,600
 But it can encode enough structure

144
00:05:57,600 --> 00:06:00,040
 to make extremely powerful predictions.

145
00:06:00,040 --> 00:06:03,040
 This gives us a useful way to think about modern AI.

146
00:06:03,040 --> 00:06:05,200
 AI doesn't store knowledge like a dictionary

147
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:06,600
 with neat definitions.

148
00:06:06,600 --> 00:06:09,440
 Instead, much of what it learns is distributed across

149
00:06:09,440 --> 00:06:11,400
 enormous mathematical spaces.

150
00:06:11,400 --> 00:06:12,920
 Ideas become patterns.

151
00:06:12,920 --> 00:06:14,800
 Relationships become directions.

152
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:16,760
 Similarity becomes distance.

153
00:06:16,760 --> 00:06:20,040
 Computation becomes movement through those spaces.

154
00:06:20,040 --> 00:06:23,480
 This is why vectors appear almost everywhere in machine learning.

155
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:25,120
 Text becomes vectors.

156
00:06:25,120 --> 00:06:26,680
 Images become vectors.

157
00:06:26,680 --> 00:06:28,160
 Audio becomes vectors.

158
00:06:28,160 --> 00:06:29,800
 Videos become vectors.

159
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:32,080
 User preferences can become vectors.

160
00:06:32,080 --> 00:06:34,600
 Even entire documents can become vectors.

161
00:06:34,600 --> 00:06:36,360
 Once something becomes a vector,

162
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:38,920
 mathematics can compare it with something else.

163
00:06:38,920 --> 00:06:41,440
 Search engines can find similar documents.

164
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:44,480
 Recommendation systems can find similar interests.

165
00:06:44,480 --> 00:06:47,440
 Image systems can connect pictures with descriptions.

166
00:06:47,440 --> 00:06:51,040
 Language models can connect one word with the context around it.

167
00:06:51,040 --> 00:06:53,240
 The vector itself isn't intelligent.

168
00:06:53,240 --> 00:06:54,680
 It's just numbers.

169
00:06:54,680 --> 00:06:57,360
 But when billions of these numerical representations

170
00:06:57,360 --> 00:07:00,560
 interact through carefully learned mathematical transformations,

171
00:07:00,560 --> 00:07:02,320
 something remarkable happens.

172
00:07:02,320 --> 00:07:04,280
 A machine begins to recognize patterns

173
00:07:04,280 --> 00:07:06,600
 that look surprisingly close to meaning.

174
00:07:06,600 --> 00:07:09,040
 So the next time you ask an AI a question,

175
00:07:09,040 --> 00:07:11,080
 remember what's happening underneath.

176
00:07:11,080 --> 00:07:12,520
 Your words disappear.

177
00:07:12,520 --> 00:07:13,840
 They become numbers.

178
00:07:13,840 --> 00:07:17,320
 Those numbers become points in enormous mathematical spaces.

179
00:07:17,320 --> 00:07:20,600
 Those points are compared, transformed, combined,

180
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:22,680
 and passed through layer after layer.

181
00:07:22,680 --> 00:07:25,800
 And eventually, numbers become language again.

182
00:07:25,800 --> 00:07:29,840
 AI may seem like magic, but deep underneath it are vectors.

